# MalloySim scenario: charged particles in a 3D magnetic field (M29).
#
# This is what M29 adds, and it is motion two dimensions cannot express. In 2D
# a magnetic field was a scalar, out of the plane, and it turned a particle in a
# flat circle. In 3D the field is a VECTOR with a direction, and q(v x B) turns
# only the part of the velocity ACROSS the field while leaving the part ALONG it
# untouched. So a particle circles across the field and drifts along it at the
# same time: a helix. That combination has no 2D form.
#
# As in 2D, a magnetic force does no work, so it cannot change speed. The turn
# is integrated exactly, as a rotation about the field direction, so the |v|
# column does not move to any digit printed, even though the particle now also
# has a velocity component along the field that a wrong axis of rotation would
# corrupt.

type charges3d

dt 0.0062831853071795865
steps 2000
output_every 500

# Field alone, no pairwise interaction, so two particles may share a start
# point without a Coulomb blow-up.
coulomb 0.0

# The field points along z. A charge circles in the xy plane and drifts in z.
bfield3 0.0 0.0 1.0

# charge3 <mass> <q> <px> <py> <pz> <vx> <vy> <vz>

# A 3-4-5 velocity: 3 across the field, 4 along it. The cyclotron radius is
# m v_perp / (|q| b) = 3, the period is 2*pi*m / (|q| b) = 2*pi = 1000 steps, and
# the speed is exactly 5. It traces a helix of radius 3 climbing in z at 4.
charge3 1.0  1.0   0.0 0.0 0.0   3.0 0.0 4.0

# A velocity purely ALONG the field: v x B = 0, so no force at all. It moves in
# a straight line up the z axis at speed 3, which is the other end of the same
# fact, that the field only turns the perpendicular part.
charge3 1.0  1.0   3.0 0.0 0.0   0.0 0.0 3.0

# Expected, from a real run of the shipped binary:
#
#   step      0   E   1.70000000e+01   |v|  5.00000000e+00   |p|  ...
#   step   2000   E   1.70000000e+01   |v|  5.00000000e+00   |p|  ...
#
# The invariants, exact rather than close:
#
# 1. |v| of the first particle does not move: a magnetic force does no work, and
#    the rotation that implements it cannot change a length. That the length now
#    includes a component ALONG the field (the 4 in the 3-4-5) is the point: a
#    rotation about the wrong axis would mix it into the perpendicular part and
#    change the perpendicular circling, and a naive kick would grow |v| every
#    step.
#
# 2. E is purely kinetic here (no field potential, no pairwise term):
#    0.5*1*25 + 0.5*1*9 = 12.5 + 4.5 = 17, exactly what step 0 prints.
#
# 3. Total momentum along z does not move: each particle keeps its own velocity
#    component along the field (4 and 3), so p_z = 4 + 3 = 7 forever. This is the
#    drift half of the helix, the thing 2D has no room for.
#
# On the ASCII view (an orthographic projection down z, so the drift is into the
# screen): the first particle traces a circle of radius 3, the second sits still
# at (3, 0) because its motion is straight along the viewing axis.

# Machine-checkable assertions, evaluated against a real run by the scenario
# tests. `step 0` is the state before any step, and the tolerance is absolute.
# check step 0    energy      1.70000000e+01   tol 1e-9
# check step 2000 energy      1.70000000e+01   tol 1e-9
# check step 2000 speed       5.00000000e+00   tol 1e-10
# check step 0    momentum_z  7.00000000e+00   tol 1e-12
# check step 2000 momentum_z  7.00000000e+00   tol 1e-9
